  "answer": "CDK5-Dependent NUP98 Phosphorylation Initiates Transport Failure in TARDBP-Mutant Motor Neurons\n\nHypothesis. In TARDBP-mutant motor neurons lacking a C9orf72 repeat expansion, sustained CDK5-dependent phosphorylation of NUP98 is a required initiating step in nuclear-import failure: phosphorylation reduces NUP98 residence at nuclear pores, import declines, nuclear TDP-43 becomes depleted, and persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies subsequently amplify transport damage and neuronal dysfunction. Here, “initiating” means initiating this transport-collapse sequence—not causing the inherited mutation or explaining all ALS.\n\nSupported premises. Disease-linked TDP-43 can disrupt nucleoporin localization and nuclear transport, and pore pathology occurs in ALS tissue [S1]. Persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies can accompany transport-factor mislocalization, impaired import, nuclear TDP-43 depletion, and cell death without conventional stress granules [S3]. Alternatively, stress granules can sequester transport factors and impair transport [S4]. C9orf72 expansion models show altered RanGAP localization and impaired import [S2], supporting restriction to a defined genetic context rather than assuming one universal pathway. None of these sources establishes NUP98 phosphorylation as an upstream cause; phosphorylated TDP-43 in S3 is not evidence for phosphorylated NUP98.\n\nSpeculative bridges. Candidate selection draws on unverified prior knowledge concerning NUP98 phosphoregulation and neuronal CDK5 signaling. The proposed CDK5–NUP98 substrate relationship, phosphorylation-dependent loss of pore attachment, and ordering before persistent TDP-43 assemblies are assumptions. The relevant NUP98 residues are unknown; they must be measured rather than invented. The hypothesis also assumes that reduced pore-associated NUP98 impairs import sufficiently to contribute to neuronal dysfunction, rather than merely marking broader cellular injury.\n\nTemporal test. Use human induced-pluripotent-stem-cell-derived motor neurons from multiple TARDBP-mutant backgrounds with matched gene-corrected controls, all without C9orf72 expansions. Sample densely before and during emerging dysfunction. Measure CDK5 activity, endogenous NUP98 phosphorylation by phosphoproteomics, NUP98 pore residence, kinetic nuclear-import reporters, endogenous TDP-43 localization, persistent cytoplasmic assemblies, conventional stress granules, neuronal firing, and survival. Measure export, passive leakage, cellular energy status, and membrane integrity to distinguish import failure from nonspecific deterioration. The predicted order is increased NUP98 phosphorylation, reduced pore residence, impaired import, nuclear TDP-43 depletion, then persistent assemblies and functional decline. Temporal precedence alone would not establish causality.\n\nPerturbational test. Identify CDK5-sensitive NUP98 phosphosites in discovery cultures, then lock the site set before independent validation. Confirm direct phosphorylation with a purified kinase–substrate assay. Introduce endogenous phosphorylation-resistant NUP98 substitutions and sequence-reverted controls; require comparable baseline NUP98 abundance, pore localization, and transport. Test whether these substitutions preserve import, nuclear TDP-43, and neuronal function despite continued CDK5 activation and matched mutant TDP-43 expression. Independent genetic CDK5 reduction provides an upstream perturbation. For sufficiency, calibrated CDK5 activation in corrected neurons should produce verified NUP98 phosphorylation and early import failure before detectable assemblies, with these effects prevented by phosphorylation-resistant NUP98. Phosphomimetic substitutions would provide only supporting evidence because they do not reproduce an actual phosphate group.\n\nDiscriminating alternatives. Compare NUP98 protection with independently validated suppression of conventional stress-granule formation and persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assembly. Granule suppression alone cannot exclude the stress-granule-independent route in S3. If assembly suppression prevents transport failure and NUP98 phosphorylation appears only afterward, sequestration or secondary stress is favored. Conversely, early protection by phosphorylation-resistant NUP98 while stress indicators remain comparable supports intrinsic pore modification upstream of assembly-associated damage. Broad CDK5 effects remain an alternative unless protection specifically depends on the mapped NUP98 sites.\n\nFalsifier and limitations. A decisive falsifying observation would be unchanged early import failure and nuclear TDP-43 depletion despite confirmed elimination of the proposed NUP98 phosphorylation, normal baseline pore function, and matched mutant TDP-43 expression. Site substitutions may themselves alter protein structure, and undetected small assemblies could undermine apparent temporal precedence. Cultured motor neurons incompletely represent aging and multicellular ALS pathology. Positive results would establish a context-specific preclinical mechanism, not a validated treatment or a general cause of ALS.",
